    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Cusco was occupied by the Killke people from about 900, became the capital of the Kingdom of Cusco and then of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca Empire</a>, and was planned round two channelled rivers, four quarters and roads to the four provinces.</li><li>The Spanish entered the city in November 1533, plundered its palaces and temples and built a new city on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> walls; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manco-inca">Manco Inca</a> besieged it for ten months in 1536 and failed.</li><li>Earthquakes in 1650 and 1950 destroyed much of the colonial city, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> walls that withstood them are now visible again.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>When the earthquake of 1950 damaged more than a third of the city's structures, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> architecture withstood it, and the damage exposed granite retaining walls of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coricancha">Qurikancha</a> that had been hidden.</p></aside>
    </section>

