    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1100</span><span class="rf-text">The Killke build the walled complex of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sacsayhuaman">Sacsayhuamán</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1532</span><span class="rf-text">Generals of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atahualpa">Atahualpa</a> capture Cusco from the supporters of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huascar">Huáscar</a> at the Battle of Quipaipan</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1533</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-pizarro">Francisco Pizarro</a> arrives in Cusco on 15 November</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1534</span><span class="rf-text">The Spanish re-establish the city on 23 March</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1536</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manco-inca">Manco Inca</a> besieges Cusco for ten months and then retreats to Vilcabamba</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1650</span><span class="rf-text">An earthquake destroys almost all the colonial buildings</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1781</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tupac-amaru-ii">Túpac Amaru II</a> besieges Cusco and is later executed in the Plaza de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arma-people">Armas</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1950</span><span class="rf-text">An earthquake on 21 May damages more than a third of the city's structures</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/manco-capac"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/manco-capac.jpg" alt="Manco Capac" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/manco-capac">Manco Capac</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_Manco_Capac,_First_Inca,_1_of_14_Portraits_of_Inca_Kings.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quechua">Quechua</a> name of the city is Qusqu. Although it was used in Southern Quechua, the name is traced to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aymara">Aymara</a> phrase qusqu wanka, "rock of the owl", which is tied to the foundation myth of the Ayar siblings. In one account of the myth, Ayar Awqa (Ayar Auca) was turned into a rock to mark his lineage's possession of the land, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manco-capac">Manco Capac</a> and his companion, with four women, built a house and planted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a> taken from the cave called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pacaritambo">Pacaritambo</a>, "those of origin". The Spanish adopted the local name and wrote it Cuzco or, less often, Cozco; the form Cusco was also used in colonial times.</p><p>The city stands in the central Andes between the Huatanay and Urubamba rivers, at around 3,400 metres, with the Vilcabamba range to the north. The Killke people occupied the region from 900 to 1200 CE, before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> arrived in the 13th century. Carbon-14 dating of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sacsayhuaman">Saksaywaman</a>, the walled complex outside Cusco, shows that the Killke built it about 1100. In March 2008 archaeologists found the ruins of a temple, a roadway and an aqueduct system there, with 11 rooms thought to have held idols and mummies. By tradition the city was founded by Manco Cápac as the capital of the Kingdom of Cusco, in the 12th century by one dating.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="growth">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Growth &amp; Great Ages</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/pachacuti"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pachacuti.jpg" alt="Pachacuti" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/pachacuti">Pachacuti</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_Pachacuti,_Tenth_Inca,_1_of_14_Portraits_of_Inca_Kings_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> legend credits <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sapa-inca">Sapa Inca</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pachacuti">Pachacuti</a> with rebuilding Cusco and turning the Kingdom of Cusco from a sleepy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-state</a> into the empire of Tawantinsuyu. Archaeological evidence, however, points to a slower, more organic growth that began before Pachacuti. The city was built to a definite plan, with two rivers channelled around it, and archaeologists have suggested that the plan was repeated at other sites. Many believe that Cusco was laid out as the effigy of a puma, a sacred animal. How it was built, and how its large stones were quarried and moved, remain undetermined. Its streets followed the orientation of the sun, and its builders kept to the shape of the land.</p><p>Under the Inca the city had two sectors, hurin and hanan, which together covered the four provinces of Chinchasuyu, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antisuyu">Antisuyu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuntisuyu">Kuntisuyu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/collasuyu">Qullasuyu</a>; a road led from each quarter of the city to the matching quarter of the empire. Each local leader had to build a house in Cusco and live there part of the year, in the quarter matching the one where he held territory. After Pachacuti, an Inca's title passed to one son and his property to a corporation of his other relatives, so each new title holder had to build a new house and add new lands. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coricancha">Qurikancha</a>, "golden place", was the chief sanctuary of the Sun god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inti">Inti</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sacsayhuaman">Sacsayhuamán</a> was expanded by the Inca.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rulers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Who Ruled It</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/atahualpa"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/atahualpa.jpg" alt="Atahualpa" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/atahualpa">Atahualpa</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Atahuallpa,_Inca_XIIII_From_Berlin_Ethnologisches_Museum,_Staatliche_Museen,_Berlin,_Germany.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">anonymous, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Cusco was the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca Empire</a>, which is dated from the 13th century to 1532, and before that of the Kingdom of Cusco. After the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huayna-capac">Huayna Capac</a> in 1528 the city fell to the sphere of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huascar">Huáscar</a> in the Inca civil war. Generals of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atahualpa">Atahualpa</a> captured it in April 1532 at the Battle of Quipaipan. The first three Spaniards arrived in May 1533, after the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cajamarca">Cajamarca</a>, to collect gold for Atahualpa's ransom. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-pizarro">Francisco Pizarro</a> officially arrived on 15 November 1533 and gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manco-inca">Manco Inca</a> the Inca fringe as the new leader. Alcaldes and regidores were named on 24 March 1534, among them Gonzalo and Juan Pizarro, and the city was re-established on 23 March 1534. Pizarro left a garrison of 90 men.</p><p>Pizarro chose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> as the capital of the new territories because it was close to the sea, and Cusco lost prominence, though it was the first city of the viceroyalty to have a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>. It was later the capital of a corregimiento, of the Intendancy of Cusco and, towards the end of the viceroyalty, of the Royal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/audiencia">Audiencia</a> of Cusco. Independence was declared in 1821, but Cusco kept its loyalty to the King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> until 1824 and was the last colonial capital, under Viceroy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-de-la-serna">José de la Serna</a>. After the defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayacucho">Ayacucho</a> was known, the city council accepted the capitulation on 22 December 1824 and recognised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agustin-gamarra">Agustín Gamarra</a> as prefect; patriot troops entered on 25 December. Cusco has always been the capital of its department.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="life">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Life in the City</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/cusco-school"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cusco-school.jpg" alt="Cusco School" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/cusco-school">Cusco School</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Virgin_of_Carmel_Saving_Souls_in_Purgatory_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Cusco was long an important centre of the indigenous peoples, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quechua">Quechua</a> people still live in the city. Spanish observers wrote that the capital astonished them by the length and regularity of its streets, that the great square was surrounded by palaces, and that in delicacy of stonework the natives far excelled the Spaniards. Under Spanish rule it was the centre of colonial rule and of the spread of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> in the Andean world, and it prospered from agriculture, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> raising, mining and trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. The Spanish built churches, convents, a cathedral, a university and an archdiocese. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> Vicente de Valverde built his cathedral facing the plaza.</p><p>The colonial city grew on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> foundations. The first cathedral, the Iglesia del Triunfo, was built in 1539 on the foundations of the palace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viracocha-inca">Viracocha Inca</a>; the main cathedral, built between 1560 and 1664, used red granite blocks taken from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sacsayhuaman">Saksaywaman</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> church, begun in 1576, stands on the site of the Amarucancha, the palace of Wayna Qhapaq, and San Blas has the oldest parish church in Cusco, built in 1563. The street Hatun Rumiyuq, "the one with the big stone", holds the Stone of Twelve <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angles">Angles</a> and the palace of Inca Roca, later the Archbishop's residence. The city developed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco-school">Cuzco School</a> of painting. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> is the most common religion, the Inca religion is also practised by the Quechua people, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inti-raymi">Inti Raymi</a> is held on 24 June at Sacsayhuamán.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="crises">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sieges &amp; Disasters</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/manco-inca"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/manco-inca.jpg" alt="Manco Inca" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/manco-inca">Manco Inca</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SAHUARAURA_p108_MANCO_INCA.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Spanish plundered the palaces and religious buildings, including the royal mummies in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coricancha">Coricancha</a>, and destroyed many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> buildings, temples and palaces, using the remaining walls as the bases of a new city. Pizarro is said to have sacked much of the Inca city in 1535. The Qurikancha was destroyed, and the Convent of Santo Domingo was built on it. In the siege of 1536 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manco-inca">Manco Inca Yupanqui</a> took control of the city from the Spanish. The siege lasted ten months and failed; his forces held the city for only a few days. He withdrew to Vilcabamba, capital of the small Neo-Inca State, which survived another 36 years, and he never returned. Many Incas died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a> epidemics, having no acquired immunity.</p><p>In 1650 a violent earthquake destroyed almost all the colonial buildings, and the effigy of the Lord of the Earthquakes, still carried in procession each year, gained great importance. In 1781 the army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tupac-amaru-ii">Túpac Amaru II</a> besieged the city during the rebellion against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bourbon-reforms">Bourbon Reforms</a>. He was taken prisoner and executed with his entire family in the Plaza de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arma-people">Armas</a>, though the rebellion went on until 1783. The Cuzco Rebellion of 1814 was put down in less than a year. A second earthquake on 21 May 1950 damaged more than a third of the city's structures; another had struck nine years earlier. A decline in the 19th century is tied to the emigration of Spanish families and the loss of trade, and the population was 47,000 in the 1840s.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/sacsayhuaman"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sacsayhuaman.jpg" alt="Sacsayhuamán" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/sacsayhuaman">Sacsayhuamán</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sacsayhuam%C3%A1n,_Cusco,_Per%C3%BA,_2015-07-31,_DD_27.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Diego Delso, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Cusco was built on layers of cultures: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> buildings stood on Killke structures, and Spanish churches and mansions replaced Inca temples and palaces. Much of the Inca masonry is visible today. After the 1950 earthquake the granite retaining walls of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coricancha">Qurikancha</a> were exposed, and restoration at the Santo Domingo complex uncovered Inca masonry without damaging the colonial building. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco-school">Cuzco School</a> of painting is the first school established in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, and its pictures fill the cathedral and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> church, among them a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/last-supper">Last Supper</a> in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> and the apostles eat guinea pig. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quechua">Quechua</a> festival <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inti-raymi">Inti Raymi</a> continues on the esplanade of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sacsayhuaman">Sacsayhuamán</a>.</p><p>The city's spelling is not fixed: in 1976 the mayor ordered the spelling Cusco in municipal publications, and in 1990 local authorities adopted Qosqo, which later administrations did not follow. The Constitution of Peru of 1993 names Cusco the Historical Capital of Peru, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> declared it a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a> in 1983. It is the capital of the Cusco Province and Department, the seat of an archbishop, and the base for visiting Inca sites such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ollantaytambo">Ollantaytambo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/machu-picchu">Machu Picchu</a>. Several hundred thousand people live in the city today.</p></div><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">Songs in history</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/songs/himno-de-riego">Himno de Riego<span>1820</span></a></p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Cusco is a city in the Peruvian Andes where the Killke people built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sacsayhuaman">Sacsayhuamán</a> about 1100 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> placed their capital. The Spanish entered it in 1533 and built a new city on Inca walls, and earthquakes in 1650 and 1950 uncovered those walls again.</em></p>

